<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-pipeline-overview">
  <title>Pipeline Overview</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The short-answer pipeline is an auditable sequence for turning imported response
      records into structured proposals for researcher review. Its five computational
      stages are <term>ingest</term>, <term>screen</term>, <term>extract</term>,
      <term>match</term>, and <term>dispose</term>. Each stage has a limited job and
      preserves a connection to the response and run that produced its output.
    </p>
    <p>
      This chapter gives the stable conceptual map. It distinguishes the deterministic
      baseline that exists in the repository from the richer Episode Model analysis the
      project intends to support. Procedures, interface details, and a field-by-field
      execution trace belong in the following chapters.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-pipeline-purpose">
    <title>Purpose and Boundaries</title>

    <p>
      The pipeline organizes responses, proposes structured analyses, preserves the
      evidence behind those proposals, and routes cases according to their review
      needs. Separating the work into stages makes an error local: a reviewer can tell
      whether a questionable result began with screening, extraction, matching, or
      disposition instead of receiving one unexplained final label.
    </p>

    <p>
      The pipeline is not an autonomous coder. It does not establish a student's
      complete conception from one response, make final research decisions that cannot
      be reviewed, silently complete missing reasoning, or erase ambiguity. A response
      is evidence of the justification visible in that response, subject to its
      question context. The interpretive boundary is defined in
      <xref ref="subsec-project-boundaries" text="title"/>; the analytic unit is the
      episode defined in <xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Pipeline output therefore remains a proposal even when the current disposer uses
      the routing label <c>auto_accept</c>. That label means that the deterministic
      cascade found one clean candidate match; it does not transfer authority from the
      researcher to the software or turn the candidate into an unreviewable finding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-pipeline-current-target">
    <title>Current Implementation and Analytic Target</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-current">
      <title>What Runs Now</title>

      <p>
        The repository contains a working SQLite-backed short-answer service. A shared
        import reader captures DataShop tabular events, the format the command-line
        interface supports; the short-answer derive step normalizes eligible event rows
        into responses. The
        four analysis stages then write screening, chain, candidate-match, and
        disposition records under a pipeline run. Implementations are replaceable
        behind small stage interfaces, but the implementations supplied now are
        deterministic baselines:
        <ul>
          <li>a rule-based screener using response length, mathematical notation, and
          connector heuristics;</li>
          <li>a keyword extractor that splits on one explicit connector and produces
          at most one coarse chain step;</li>
          <li>a matcher that applies keyword patterns and codebook surface markers,
          while deliberately ignoring structural patterns not yet supported; and</li>
          <li>a rule-based disposition cascade based on screening, extraction, OOV,
          and match results.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The stored extraction schema predates the canonical Episode Model. It names an
        extracted response a <c>chain</c> and stores ordered <c>chain_steps</c> with
        <c>premise</c>, <c>conclusion</c>, and <c>oov_flag</c>. These are literal legacy
        field names, not a competing analytic vocabulary. At present a stored premise
        is the closest available field for a criterion and a stored conclusion is the
        closest available field for a judgment; there is no separate stored
        inferential-link text or link-status field. The current baseline therefore
        cannot represent the full <m>e_k=(C_k,I_k,J_k)</m> structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The database also contains reviewer, review-code, and append-only override-log
        tables. Those records can associate a reviewer and rationale with
        <c>confirm</c>, <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>, <c>replace</c>, or
        <c>reject_all</c> code changes. They establish an audit structure; they should
        not be mistaken for a complete, currently integrated review experience across
        every stage.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-target">
      <title>Intended Analytic Behavior</title>

      <p>
        The analytic target is more expressive than the baseline. Extraction should
        propose every identifiable episode in order, preserve the student's criterion
        and judgment, classify the inferential link as explicit, implicit, or absent,
        and retain uncertainty instead of inventing a connector. Matching should use
        the question-specific working codebook, preserve multiple and alternate
        matches, and distinguish OOV language from a valid term that yields no code
        match. Correctness should remain local to criterion correctness and link
        validity, while goal coverage remains a separate response-level judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        This target is a statement of intended analytic behavior, not a description of
        features already supplied by the keyword baseline. The coding rules are
        authoritative in <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/> and
        <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>. The governed relationship
        between the shared code library and a question-specific working codebook is in
        <xref ref="ch-codebook" text="title"/>. In particular, the
        policy for nested episodes remains open and must not be settled by pipeline
        convenience.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-pipeline-stages">
    <title>The Five Stages</title>

    <p>
      The following subsections use the canonical stage names. For each stage,
      <term>preserved provenance</term> means the identifiers or explanations that let
      a later reader reconnect an output to its input; it does not imply that every
      desired research version is already stored.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-ingest">
      <title>Stage 1: Ingest</title>

      <p>
        <term>Purpose.</term> Ingest preserves the source export and produces stable,
        normalized records that later services can analyze. The shared reader first
        captures log rows as events. For events selected as short-answer data, the
        derive step creates one response record with a stable hash-derived
        <c>response_id</c>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Input and output.</term> The supported ingest format is the DataShop
        tab-separated export. A Runestone use-info CSV reader also exists and can be
        selected explicitly, but it remains a dormant seam rather than a supported
        path. Their
        events provide source fields such as
        student, problem, action text, timestamp, and course. The short-answer output
        retains the import identifier, student and question identifiers, response text,
        submission time, and course identifier. Text normalization preserves internal
        whitespace while applying Unicode normalization and removing trailing
        whitespace.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Provenance, limitation, and escalation.</term> The raw-import record keeps
        the source path, SHA-256 file hash, detected format, import time, row count, and
        notes; events and responses retain the import link. Duplicate source files are
        rejected by hash, and re-derivation skips already present response identities.
        Missing identifiers or timestamps and malformed timestamps are logged and
        counted as malformed rather than guessed. Ingest does not interpret the
        mathematics, decide codability, or de-identify data by itself. Authorization
        and identifier handling remain researcher responsibilities under
        <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-screen">
      <title>Stage 2: Screen</title>

      <p>
        <term>Purpose.</term> Screen proposes whether a response contains enough visible
        effort to proceed. It receives normalized response text and writes one
        <c>INCLUDE</c>, <c>EXCLUDE</c>, or <c>FLAG</c> decision per response and run,
        together with a heuristic confidence and rationale.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Current implementation.</term> The supplied <c>rules-v1</c> baseline
        excludes blanks, no-effort boilerplate, bare yes/no answers, and some very
        short unsupported answers. It includes text with a recognized connector. It
        flags mathematical work without a connector and longer borderline prose. Its
        confidence is explicitly a heuristic, not a calibrated probability.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Provenance, limitation, and escalation.</term> The screening row retains
        <c>response_id</c> and <c>run_id</c>, so its label, confidence, and rationale can
        be inspected with the original response and run configuration. Keyword and
        length rules cannot determine analytic relevance reliably in every question
        context. <c>FLAG</c> is therefore an explicit request for review; under the
        current disposer, both <c>FLAG</c> and <c>EXCLUDE</c> route to
        <c>escalate</c> rather than disappearing from the record.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-extract">
      <title>Stage 3: Extract</title>

      <p>
        <term>Purpose.</term> Extract proposes internal reasoning structure for screened
        responses. It receives response text plus the run's controlled-vocabulary
        version and writes a chain record, ordered steps, extractor notes, and an OOV
        flag when a clause cannot be mapped to that vocabulary.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Current implementation.</term> The supplied <c>keywords-v1</c> extractor
        finds the first supported connector, assigns the clauses on its two sides to
        the legacy <c>premise</c> and <c>conclusion</c> fields, and attempts to map both
        clauses to canonical vocabulary terms. With no connector, or with a connector
        that leaves no conclusion clause, it writes an empty chain and an explanatory
        note. When only the conclusion side survives, it records a single
        observation-only step whose stored premise is empty. With a usable connector
        it produces no more than one step. It does not infer implicit links or segment
        a response into a rich sequence of episodes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Provenance, limitation, and escalation.</term> A chain retains its
        response and run identifiers; each step retains its one-based order, and raw
        unmatched clause text is preserved rather than discarded. Empty chains later
        escalate. Any OOV step later receives <c>terminology_flag</c>. Because one OOV
        flag currently covers either side of a step, the baseline cannot identify
        which analytic component caused the vocabulary issue. Note also that the
        screener recognizes a longer connector list than the extractor splits on:
        the screener additionally accepts <c>given</c>, <c>if</c>, <c>when</c>,
        <c>gives</c>, and <c>results in</c>, and matches the bare <c>means</c>
        where the extractor splits only on <c>means that</c>. A response included
        on one of those screener-only connectors yields a no-connector note, an
        empty chain, and an eventual <c>escalate</c> disposition. The pattern is
        systematic and expected, not a data defect.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-match">
      <title>Stage 4: Match</title>

      <p>
        <term>Purpose.</term> Match compares each stored step with the selected codebook
        version and records candidate control-structure codes. It receives the legacy
        premise and conclusion plus codebook patterns and surface markers. It outputs
        zero, one, or several match rows, each with a code identifier, codebook-version
        identifier, and rationale naming the evidence that fired.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Current implementation.</term> The <c>keywords-v1</c> matcher performs
        case-insensitive substring matching for patterns explicitly classified as
        <c>keyword</c>, and separately checks semicolon-delimited surface markers. It
        retains every distinct code that fires and combines the rationales when more
        than one pattern supports the same code; for surface markers it keeps only the
        longest phrase that fired for a given code. Structural <c>slot=term</c>
        patterns are stored but are not evaluated by this baseline.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Provenance, limitation, and escalation.</term> Matches link through their
        step to the chain, response, and run and directly record the codebook version.
        Literal substring hits are candidate evidence, not proof of the student's
        meaning or of mathematical correctness. Multiple candidate codes are preserved
        for standard review, and the absence of any candidate is preserved as a
        <c>no_match</c> outcome at disposition rather than being forced into the
        nearest code. Human treatment of
        alternate interpretations and OOV cases follows
        <xref ref="subsec-decision-matching" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-pipeline-dispose">
      <title>Stage 5: Dispose</title>

      <p>
        <term>Purpose.</term> Dispose summarizes the earlier proposals into a routing
        decision. It receives the response's screening label, chain presence and step
        counts, OOV count, and distinct matched codes. It writes one disposition and a
        trigger note for that response and run.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Current implementation.</term> The supplied <c>rules-v1</c> cascade routes
        <c>EXCLUDE</c>, <c>FLAG</c>, missing chains, and empty chains to
        <c>escalate</c>; any OOV step to <c>terminology_flag</c>; no candidate codes to
        <c>no_match</c>; several codes to <c>standard_review</c>; and exactly one clean
        code to <c>auto_accept</c>. The schema also permits
        <c>problem_type_flag</c>, but the current rule baseline does not produce it
        because it does not yet perform the required question-type lookup.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Provenance, limitation, and escalation.</term> The disposition retains the
        response and run identifiers and a concise explanation of the first rule that
        fired. This is queue organization, not an analytic verdict. A coarse cascade
        cannot judge episode completeness, link status, mathematical correctness,
        question-goal coverage, or the adequacy of nested-episode coding. Those remain
        human decisions even when the route is <c>auto_accept</c>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-pipeline-human-layer">
    <title>Human Review Across the Pipeline</title>

    <p>
      Human review is a standing layer, not Stage 6. Researchers act before a run by
      governing access, defining the question frame, freezing versions, and deciding
      what counts as acceptable evidence. During and after a run, they can inspect the
      source response, challenge a screening label, revise segmentation or component
      interpretation, assess candidate matches, evaluate correctness and coverage, and
      leave a case unresolved.
    </p>

    <p>
      Review should begin at the earliest questionable transformation. A plausible
      code match cannot repair an incorrect screen or a fabricated extraction. The
      current schema supports reviewer identity, review status and timestamps, human
      code assignments, correctness digits, notes, and append-only override events.
      Each override event preserves tool codes, reviewer codes, an action, and an
      optional rationale. These records allow disagreement to remain visible rather
      than overwriting the proposal that prompted it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The authoritative review procedure, including 
      <xref ref="g-adjudication" text="title"/> and unresolved cases, is in 
      <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>. An unresolved record is a 
      successful preservation of uncertainty, not a pipeline failure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-pipeline-provenance">
    <title>Audit Trail and Provenance</title>

    <p>
      Reproducibility depends on joining three layers of provenance:
      <ol>
        <li><p><term>Source provenance:</term> the raw-import file path, file hash,
        format, import timestamp, row count, and notes, with imported events and
        derived responses tied to the import identifier.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Run provenance:</term> the run identifier, start and completion
        times, status, owning service, prompt version, model identifier,
        controlled-vocabulary version, codebook version, notes, and, for
        stage-specific runs,
        stage name and serialized configuration.</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Decision provenance:</term> per-stage rationales and notes,
        ordered extracted steps, every candidate match and its firing evidence,
        disposition trigger notes, and reviewer and override history.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Not every desired version is a first-class run field. In particular, the current
      run schema has no dedicated software-commit, coding-protocol, or question-frame
      version column. These values must not be claimed as automatically captured.
      Until the schema provides dedicated fields, a reproducible run should record
      them explicitly in run notes or a linked immutable artifact, following the
      version discipline in <xref ref="sec-data-reproducibility" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Provenance makes a proposal inspectable; it does not make the proposal correct.
      Researchers must preserve OOV terms, no-match and multiple-match outcomes,
      alternate interpretations, disagreement, and unresolved cases because those
      records are evidence about both the responses and the performance of the method.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
